{{define "pagename"}}lock{{end}}
{{- /* The title is static on purpose: the shell renders it into <title> before the view
       model is in scope, so a page that named its target here would fail to render. The
       account's name is in the banner and the caption, which are inside "body". */ -}}
{{define "title"}}Lock an account{{end}}
{{define "body"}}
{{- /* The banner states the verdict and nothing else. "Locked" is only ever printed when a
       backend proved the screen went down; anything else says what the daemon actually
       established, because this page is the operator's evidence and a confident wrong word
       here is worse than an awkward one. */ -}}
{{- $n := len .Rows -}}
<p class="banner">
  {{- if and (gt .Result.Locked 0) (eq .Result.Locked $n) -}}
    <strong>{{.Target}}</strong> is locked.
  {{- else if gt .Result.Locked 0 -}}
    {{.Result.Locked}} of {{$n}} of <strong>{{.Target}}</strong>'s sessions are locked;
    the rest are below.
  {{- else -}}
    <strong>{{.Target}}</strong> was not locked.
  {{- end -}}
</p>

{{- /* The sentence this whole feature exists to make possible. A raw virtual terminal
       answers "asked, cannot prove" forever, and a page that rounded that to either
       success or failure would teach an operator to distrust the only signal the machine
       has. It is also the one case where the page has to say what to DO. */ -}}
{{- if gt .Result.Unconfirmed 0 -}}
<p class="flag" role="status">
  {{.Result.Unconfirmed}} of {{$n}}
  {{if eq .Result.Unconfirmed 1}}session was{{else}}sessions were{{end}}
  asked to lock and nothing could prove the screen went down. That is not the same as
  locked, and it is not the same as failed: the daemon asked the desktop and the desktop
  gave no signal either way. A virtual machine with no lock backend installed answers this
  way every time, and it keeps answering this way until one is installed.
  {{- if gt .Result.Locked 0}} The sessions above that say <em>locked</em> are a different
  case: those were proved.{{end}}
  While an account is inside a lockout window the daemon asks again on every tick, so a
  screen that missed one request gets another. Outside a window nothing asks again, so
  look at the screen before trusting this line either way.
</p>
{{- end -}}

<table class="rows">
  <caption>Every session <strong>{{.Target}}</strong> holds on this machine, and what
    asking it to lock produced.</caption>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th scope="col">Session</th>
      <th scope="col">Where</th>
      <th scope="col">Result</th>
      <th scope="col">Detail</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    {{range .Rows}}
    <tr>
      <th scope="row">{{.Session}}</th>
      <td data-label="Where">
        {{- if .Seat}}{{.Seat}}{{else}}<span class="muted">no seat</span>{{end -}}
        {{- if .Type}} · {{.Type}}{{end -}}
      </td>
      <td data-label="Result">
        {{- if .Locked -}}
          <span class="state-held">locked</span>
        {{- else if .Unconfirmed -}}
          <span class="state-gap">unconfirmed</span>
        {{- else -}}
          <span class="state-unknown">not locked</span>
        {{- end -}}
      </td>
      <td data-label="Detail">
        {{- if .Detail}}<span class="problem">{{.Detail}}</span>{{end -}}
        {{- /* The one account where the two ways this daemon locks a screen genuinely
               point in opposite directions: the schedule will never hold it, and the
               operator just did. Both halves have to be on screen together, or the
               contradiction reads as a bug in whichever one they can see. */ -}}
        {{- /* The separator has to be in the text, not in CSS. Both actions above trim
               their surrounding whitespace, so with a detail present the two spans land
               against each other and the browser renders "...cannot confirm itThis is the
               session..." — a run-on in the one cell an operator quotes as evidence. A
               margin would separate them visually and still copy out as one word. */ -}}
        {{- if .SelfSession}}{{if .Detail}} {{end}}<span class="override">This is the session the daemon runs
          inside. Its schedule never locks it, because the daemon cannot lock itself out of
          enforcing; you asked for this one, so it happened.</span>{{end -}}
      </td>
    </tr>
    {{end}}
  </tbody>
</table>

<p><a href="/">Back to the status page</a></p>
{{end}}
